<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="F_SetGoodAttStatus" Id="{e6cd67bb-2bd9-4b3d-9199-950e5913b39f}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    F_SetGoodAttStatus - returns an attenuator status word forced into the
    "good" state used by tests.

    PURPOSE
        Test helper for PMPS test fixtures. Starting from the attenuator status
        word passed in xStatus, it sets the bits that describe a healthy,
        PMPS-governed attenuator, so code that judges attenuator health (for
        example F_AttenuatorOK) sees the attenuator as safe and stable.

    BIT LAYOUT
        The bit layout is the one declared in ST_AttenuatorStatus:
            bit 0  AtTarget : attenuator has reached its requested transmission
            bit 1  Moving   : attenuator is travelling to a new transmission
            bit 2  LocalMode: attenuator ignores PMPS requests, follows a local
                              setpoint
            bit 3  PMPSMode : attenuator takes setpoints only from PMPS
            bit 4  OK       : no attenuator error, status is stable
            bit 5  Toggle   : unused
            bit 6  Include  : consider this device's status

        This helper forces bit 0 (AtTarget), bit 3 (PMPSMode) and bit 4 (OK) to
        1 and leaves every other bit of the supplied word unchanged. Starting
        from a zeroed word the result reads "at target, in PMPS mode, no
        errors", for which F_AttenuatorOK returns TRUE.

    USAGE
        For test code only, never for production logic:
            stAttenuator.AttStatus := F_SetGoodAttStatus(stAttenuator.AttStatus);

        The modified status word is returned through the function name.
        xStatus is a by-value VAR_INPUT and is not modified by this function.

    OBJECTIVES
        - Keep the definition of a "good" attenuator status word in one place,
          so a change of bit layout is made in a single function.
        - Set the good bits on top of the caller's word, so all other bits
          (Moving, LocalMode, Include, ...) pass through unchanged.
        - Follow standard TwinCAT function structure: inputs are read-only and
          the result is returned through the function name only.

    HISTORY
        2020-11-18 / Alex Wallace
            Function created with the "Tests for the margin logic" change.
        2026-11-28 / Scirix Agent
            Fixed function structure: the result is now assigned from xStatus
            before the good bits are set, so the helper returns the modified
            status word instead of writing to its by-value input and always
            returning 0 (audit finding #1135).
*)
FUNCTION F_SetGoodAttStatus : UINT
VAR_INPUT
    xStatus : UINT; // Attenuator status word (ST_AttenuatorStatus layout); bits 0 AtTarget, 3 PMPSMode and 4 OK are forced to 1, all other bits pass through unchanged
END_VAR
VAR
    nStatus : UINT; // Working copy of xStatus in which the good bits are set; assigned to the function result at the end
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[// Build the result on a local copy of the status word: xStatus is a
// by-value VAR_INPUT and is only ever read here.
nStatus := xStatus;

nStatus.0 := TRUE; // AtTarget: at the requested transmission
nStatus.3 := TRUE; // PMPSMode: attenuator follows PMPS setpoints
nStatus.4 := TRUE; // OK: no attenuator error

// Standard function structure: the result is returned through the function
// name, never through an input.
F_SetGoodAttStatus := nStatus;]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>